  "batch_notes": "(1) rust/lz4_flex has two distinct tests with the identical id rust/lz4_flex::test_fast_short_slice_copy (src/fastcpy.rs and src/fastcpy_unsafe.rs); both are emitted here, so the id appears twice. (2) Numerical-linear-algebra 'reconstruction' properties (Cholesky/LU/QR/SVD rebuild A within a tolerance) sit between roundtrip and spec-postcondition; classified roundtrip (factor then multiply back) with medium confidence, while residual/orthonormality/eigen-equation checks went to spec-postcondition. The nalgebra tests are notable for deriving every tolerance from backward-stability bounds (Higham) and skipping on measured condition numbers; svd_reconstructs_matrix runs with a deliberately loosened sqrt(eps) 'defect ceiling' until nalgebra/1 is fixed. (3) 'Wrapper equals the primitive' tests (noisy_float; liquid arithmetic/case/trim filters vs Rust std) were classified external-oracle because the rubric lists the language stdlib as external, but they are borderline: the crate under test often delegates to that same primitive, so the oracle is not fully independent. (4) CRDT convergence (loro prop_concurrent_replicas_converge) fits neither stateful-model (no model) nor internal-consistency cleanly; classified algebraic-law (order-independence of merge). (5) Several loro/native_db pins are generative properties over a fixed scenario with one drawn value (tagged pin); native_db's pbt_f64_secondary_scan_sorted forces a failing input via its generator. (6) Bug liquid/1 has an empty test field; its only in-batch link is the depth cap in nested_blocks_parse_and_render_up_to_supported_depth. (7) Doc coverage is uneven: lodepng, loro, nalgebra and native_db READMEs list every property as '(no doc comment)' although the source has substantial comments."
